.TH module
.SH NAME
module \- a self-contained game content package
.SH DESCRIPTION
A module is the top-level container for all game content in the Flame engine.
.br
It holds module-wide resources (characters, effects, items, recipes, skills, races, trainings)
that are available across all chapters, as well as the chapters themselves.
.br
A module consists of a directory on the file system with a
.B .module
configuration file and a set of sub-directories holding the data files.
.br
Only one module can be active at a time; the engine loads it at startup and
advances through its chapters as the game progresses.
.SH STRUCTURE
.P
A module directory must contain the following:
.P
Files:
.br
.B .module
\- module configuration file (see data/file/conf/.module)
.P
Sub-directories:
.br
.B chapters
\- directory with module chapters (required; see data/dir/chapters)
.br
.B characters
\- character base data files and exported player characters (see data/dir/characters)
.br
.B effects
\- effect base data files (see effects)
.br
.B items
\- item base data files: armors, weapons, misc (see inventory-item)
.br
.B lang
\- translation files for module-level resources (see data/dir/lang)
.br
.B objects
\- area object base data files (see objects)
.br
.B races
\- race data files (lives under characters/races)
.br
.B recipes
\- crafting recipe data files (see data/dir/recipes)
.br
.B skills
\- skill base data files (see data/dir/skills)
.br
.B trainings
\- training data files
.SH CONFIGURATION
The
.B .module
file in the module root directory controls how the engine loads the module.
.P
Required values:
.br
.B id
\- unique module identifier
.br
.B chapter
\- ID of the chapter to start with
.br
.B chapters
\- semicolon-separated list of all chapter IDs in the module
.P
Optional values:
.br
.B json-data
\- set to
.I true
if data files use JSON format (default: XML)
.SH CREATING A MODULE
.P
1. Create a directory whose name matches the desired module ID.
.P
2. Create a
.B .module
file in that directory with at minimum the
.B id
and
.B chapter
keys set.
.P
3. Create the required sub-directories (chapters, characters, effects, items,
lang, recipes, skills).
.P
4. Add at least one chapter sub-directory inside
.B chapters/
with a matching
.B .chapter
configuration file and a starting area.
.P
5. Populate the resource sub-directories with data files (JSON or XML).
.P
6. Load the module in code using
.B data.ImportModuleDir
and pass the result to
.B flame.NewModule
(see EXAMPLE below).
.SH EXAMPLE
.P
Directory layout:
.nf
my-mod/
    .module
    chapters/
        prologue/
            .chapter
            areas/
    characters/
        main.json
    effects/
        main.json
    items/
        armors/
            main.json
        weapons/
            main.json
        misc/
            main.json
    lang/
        english/
            main.lang
    recipes/
        main.json
    skills/
        main.json
    trainings/
        main.json
.fi
.P
.B .module
file:
.nf
id:my-mod
chapter:prologue
chapters:prologue;chapter1;epilogue
json-data:true
.fi
.P
Loading the module in Go:
.nf
modData, err := data.ImportModuleDir("my-mod")
if err != nil {
    panic(err)
}
mod := flame.NewModule(modData)
.fi
